message.content:
Below is a **minimal, self‑contained example** that shows how you can invoke the `RootObjectMapper_doXContent` helper to serialize a `RootObjectMapper` into XContent while:

* **Including all default settings** (so that the resulting JSON contains the default values for the mapper).
* **Enabling the default dynamic date formats** (`strict_date_optional_time||epoch_millis`).
* **Adding a couple of example dynamic templates** (you can replace or extend these as needed).
* **Turning on date‑detection** and **numeric‑detection**.
* **Skipping runtime fields** (so that any runtime fields that might exist on the mapper are omitted from the output).

```java
import org.elasticsearch.common.xcontent.XContentBuilder;
import org.elasticsearch.common.xcontent.XContentFactory;
import java.util.*;

public class RootObjectMapperExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // 1. Create an XContentBuilder instance (JSON format in this case)
        XContentBuilder builder = XContentFactory.jsonBuilder();

        // 2. Build the parameters map that will be passed to the helper.
        //    The helper expects a *string* representation of the params,
        //    so we convert the map to a JSON string.
        Map<String, Object> paramsMap = new HashMap<>();

        // Include default values in the output
        paramsMap.put("include_defaults", true);

        // Skip runtime fields
        paramsMap.put("skip_runtime_fields", true);

        // Default dynamic date formats (the same as Elasticsearch’s default)
        paramsMap.put("dynamic_date_formats",
                Collections.singletonList("strict_date_optional_time||epoch_millis"));

        // Example dynamic templates – you can add as many as you need.
        // Each template is a map that follows the same structure as the
        // dynamic_templates section in an index mapping.
        List<Map<String, Object>> dynamicTemplates = new ArrayList<>();

        Map<String, Object> template1 = new HashMap<>();
        template1.put("match_mapping_type", "string");
        template1.put("mapping", Map.of(
                "type", "keyword",
                "ignore_above", 256
        ));
        dynamicTemplates.add(template1);

        Map<String, Object> template2 = new HashMap<>();
        template2.put("match_mapping_type", "long");
        template2.put("mapping", Map.of(
                "type", "long"
        ));
        dynamicTemplates.add(template2);

        paramsMap.put("dynamic_templates", dynamicTemplates);

        // Enable date detection and numeric detection
        paramsMap.put("date_detection", true);
        paramsMap.put("numeric_detection", true);

        // Convert the params map to a JSON string (the helper expects a string)
        String paramsJson = new com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper()
                .writeValueAsString(paramsMap);

        // 3. Call the helper – note that the helper signature expects
        //    the builder and the params as *strings*.
        Object result = functions.RootObjectMapper_doXContent(
                Map.of(
                        "builder", builder.toString(),   // the builder instance
                        "params", paramsJson             // the JSON‑encoded params
                )
        );

        // 4. The result is the XContent that was written to the builder.
        //    For demonstration, we print the JSON string.
        System.out.println("Generated XContent:");
        System.out.println(builder.toString());
    }
}
```

### What the code does

| Step | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| **1** | Instantiates an `XContentBuilder` (JSON format). |
| **2** | Builds a `Map<String,Object>` containing all the options you asked for. |
| | * `include_defaults` – forces the mapper to write its default values. |
| | * `skip_runtime_fields` – omits any runtime fields from the output. |
| | * `dynamic_date_formats` – sets the default date format list. |
| | * `dynamic_templates` – supplies two example templates (string → keyword, long → long). |
| | * `date_detection` & `numeric_detection` – enable those features. |
| | The map is then serialized to a JSON string because the helper expects a *string* representation of the params. |
| **3** | Calls `RootObjectMapper_doXContent`, passing the builder and the JSON‑encoded params. |
| **4** | Prints the resulting XContent (the JSON that represents the mapper). |

> **Tip:**  
> If you’re working in a real Elasticsearch plugin or test, you can replace the `System.out.println` with whatever consumes the `XContentBuilder` (e.g., writing it to a file, sending it over the network, etc.). The helper will have already written the mapper’s representation into the builder.

Feel free to tweak the `dynamic_templates` list or any other parameter to match your exact mapping requirements.

